%I A396754 #25 Sep 05 2026 21:29:33
%S A396754 0,0,1,1,2,11,36,240,1986,25259
%N A396754 Number of point-determining (also called mating, or twin-free) diameter-2-critical graphs with n nodes.
%C A396754 A graph G is diameter-2-critical if its diameter is 2 and the deletion of any edge increases the diameter (a disconnected graph having diameter infinity). Such a graph is primitive, or twin-free, if no two distinct vertices have the same open neighborhood, that is, if it has no duplicated vertex.
%C A396754 A graph in which no two distinct vertices have the same open neighborhood is called point-determining or mating in the enumeration literature, see A006024 and A004110, and primitive in Radosavljević, Stanić and Živković (2024), where these particular graphs are studied.
%C A396754 Duplicating a vertex v of a diameter-2-critical graph, that is, adding a new vertex adjacent to exactly the neighbors of v, always yields another diameter-2-critical graph, so each primitive graph generates an infinite family. The converse fails for the complete bipartite graphs: every vertex of K_{s,t} has a twin, so repeatedly deleting duplicated vertices reaches K_2, which has diameter 1 and is not diameter-2-critical. See Radosavljević, Stanić and Živković (2024).
%C A396754 The twin-free core of a diameter-2-critical graph, obtained by repeatedly deleting a duplicated vertex, is either K_2 or a point-determining diameter-2-critical graph. Hence a(n) counts exactly the diameter-2-critical graphs on n nodes that are not blow-ups of a smaller one, and A330333(n) = a(n) + T(n) where T(n) counts the blow-ups. Machine verified over all diameter-2-critical graphs through n = 12.
%H A396754 J. Radosavljević, Z. Stanić and M. Živković, <a href="https://doi.org/10.2298/PIM2429021R">Primitive diameter 2-critical graphs</a>, Publ. Inst. Math. (Beograd) 115(129) (2024), 21-32.
%e A396754 a(3) = a(4) = 0 because each of the diameter-2-critical graphs on 3 or 4 nodes has a duplicated vertex: K_{1,2} and K_{1,3} have twin leaves, and C_4 = K_{2,2} has twins on both sides. The smallest primitive example is C_5.
%e A396754 a(5) = 1: the diameter-2-critical graphs on 5 nodes are K_{1,4}, K_{2,3} and C_5, and only C_5 is twin-free.
%Y A396754 Cf. A330333 (all diameter-2-critical graphs), A001349, A004110, A006024.
